---
import Layout from "@/components/Layout.astro";
import { propagation, meta } from "@/lib/load";
import { formatDate } from "@/lib/format";
import { withBase } from "@/lib/paths";
import { FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL, REQUIREMENTS } from "@/lib/profile-labels";
import type { FieldSource } from "@/lib/profile-labels";

const sources: FieldSource[] = ["standard", "policy", "ai-plan", "tracker"];

const corpusStart = meta.corpusStart ? formatDate(meta.corpusStart) : "November 2025";

const tierCounts: Record<"added" | "present-at-start" | "tied", number> = {
  added: 0,
  "present-at-start": 0,
  tied: 0,
};
for (const c of propagation.clusters) {
  const t = c.firstObserved?.tier;
  if (t) tierCounts[t] += 1;
}
---

<Layout
  title="Reading the data"
  description="How to read the change summaries, statement profiles, adoption charts, originality figures and reuse heat-map on this site, and the limits of each."
>
  <div class="page reading prose stack">
    <h1>Reading the data</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Most Commonwealth agencies are required to publish a statement about how they
      use AI. This site keeps every version of those statements and turns the changes
      into summaries, profiles, charts and colours. This page explains what each of
      those means and what it doesn&rsquo;t claim.
    </p>

    <h2 id="corpus">When the record starts</h2>
    <p>
      Tracking began on {corpusStart}, the week the Australian Public Service
      launched its AI Plan (the <a href={withBase("/about")}>About page</a> tells
      the story). The statements themselves have been <a
        href="https://www.digital.gov.au/ai/ai-in-government-policy/standard-ai-transparency-statements"
        >required since February 2025</a
      >, so their first nine months, when agencies were drawing their wording from
      one another, came before the tracker started. Every statement that already
      existed enters the record on day one, marked &ldquo;first tracked&rdquo;,
      because we don&rsquo;t know when it was published or what it looked like
      before then. That gap limits much of the analysis below.
    </p>

    <h2 id="reuse">Shared passages and the reuse heat-map</h2>
    <p>
      Agencies reuse one another&rsquo;s wording heavily. On each statement page,
      passages carry two different tints. An <em>amber</em> tint means the same
      passage appears in other agencies&rsquo; statements; the deeper the amber, the
      more widely it is shared:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>uncoloured</strong>: unique to that statement;</li>
      <li><strong>a few</strong>: shared with two or three other agencies;</li>
      <li><strong>several</strong>: shared with four to nine;</li>
      <li><strong>many</strong>: shared with ten to twenty-four;</li>
      <li><strong>most</strong>: shared with twenty-five or more.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      An amber passage also carries a small count; open it to see which other
      agencies use the same words, each linked to its statement.
    </p>
    <p>
      A separate <em>cool grey</em> tint marks <em>template wording</em>: phrasing
      taken straight from the Policy or the Digital Transformation Agency&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://www.digital.gov.au/ai/ai-in-government-policy/standard-ai-transparency-statements"
        >template</a
      >, which most agencies start from. Template wording is boilerplate that
      can&rsquo;t be traced to particular agencies, so it gets its own colour and
      has no count to open.
    </p>
    <h3 id="first-observed">First observed in our corpus</h3>
    <p>
      For every shared passage we also record which tracked statement showed it
      earliest, and the order in which the others picked it up. Each passage falls into
      one of three tiers, according to how far that ordering can be trusted:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>added during tracking</strong> ({tierCounts.added}): we watched the
        first agency add the passage well after the record opened, with others
        following later. This is the strongest evidence of order.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>present at the start</strong> ({tierCounts["present-at-start"]}): the
        first agency already had the passage when tracking began, and others adopted it
        later. We can order the latecomers, but not that first agency&rsquo;s own source.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>tied</strong> ({tierCounts.tied}): several agencies share the earliest
        date, usually because they were all present on day one, so we make no claim
        about order.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Read this as the order <em>we</em> observed, which is no proof of who wrote a
      passage first. A passage two agencies both carried on day one can&rsquo;t be
      ordered at all, and even a clean ordering says only who we saw with it first.
      The real source could be an agency we don&rsquo;t track, an internal draft
      that never went online, or the DTA template itself.
    </p>

    <h2 id="originality">Originality scores</h2>
    <p>
      A statement&rsquo;s originality score is the share of its text, by length, that
      is <em>neither</em> shared with other agencies <em>nor</em> drawn from the
      template. A statement at 80% is mostly in its own words; one at 20% is mostly
      shared or templated language. The leaderboard and the agency grid colour each
      statement on this scale, from borrowed to bespoke.
    </p>
    <p>
      The score measures how much of a statement&rsquo;s text appears elsewhere,
      and it is no grade of effort. The DTA scores low because it publishes the
      template, so almost everything it says is shared by definition; the site
      labels it as the template source. Two other things lower a score for reasons
      unrelated to effort. Shared text includes wording the Standard
      itself calls for (the document&rsquo;s title, its section headings, the OECD
      definition of an AI system that the policy adopts), so an agency that follows the
      Standard to the letter shares more than one that ignores it. And a statement
      published jointly by two bodies (the AASB and AUASB, for instance) counts as
      shared with itself.
    </p>

    <h2 id="changes">How changes are read</h2>
    <p>
      The scrape records every change to a statement&rsquo;s text, and most of those
      changes have nothing to do with what the agency says: a rotating sidebar, a
      &ldquo;last reviewed 3 days ago&rdquo; counter ticking over, a page rebuilt with
      the same words. So every revision is classified from its diff. Mechanical
      rules settle the obvious cases (formatting only, links only, page chrome, a
      date stamp, passages reordered). A language model (Claude) reads the rest,
      sorting each diff into one of five kinds and writing a one-sentence summary in
      plain English:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>scrape noise</strong>: nothing the agency wrote changed;</li>
      <li><strong>cosmetic</strong>: wording, typos, formatting, link targets, a date;</li>
      <li>
        <strong>expanded</strong>: the same substance at more length, such as a bullet
        grown into a paragraph or an example added to an existing claim;
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>restructured</strong>: reorganised or reworded, claims and commitments
        substantially the same;
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>substantive</strong>: a claim, commitment, fact or disclosure added,
        removed or altered.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      By default only the last three kinds appear on the timeline and in the story on
      each statement page. Of those, only the substantive kind counts as a
      <strong>change of substance</strong>. Expanded and restructured revisions are
      shown because the statement now reads differently, but they aren&rsquo;t
      counted, since by definition their claims and commitments stayed the same. A
      revision that nothing has read yet (rare: no rule matched and no model was
      available) is shown as <strong>unclassified</strong> and isn&rsquo;t counted
      until it is read.
    </p>
    <p>
      The model is told to report removals as well as additions, that an expansion
      keeping the same substance is never substantive, and that text lost because a
      capture was cut short is scrape noise. Every summary names the model that
      wrote it and sits next to the raw diff, so you can check its reading against
      the words.
    </p>
    <p>
      Outside the model, a capture we know to be broken is dropped before anything
      reads it, and one that suddenly loses most of its text is held off the site
      until confirmed. Dropping a capture can delay the date we observe a genuine
      change by a few weeks, a cost we accept to avoid recording false changes.
    </p>

    <h2 id="profiles">Statement profiles</h2>
    <p>
      Each revision of each statement is also read into a <em>profile</em>: a fixed
      set of questions, each with a fixed vocabulary of answers. The questions ask
      which DTA usage patterns and domains the statement says are in use, and whether
      it addresses public-facing AI and commits to a human intermediary. Others ask
      whether it names an accountable official, the status of its Chief AI Officer
      (in place, planned or not mentioned), and whether it mentions a use-case
      register or mandatory training. The last few record how often it promises to
      be reviewed, when it says it was last updated, and what it explicitly promises
      not to do.
    </p>
    <h3 id="schema">Where the questions come from</h3>
    <p>
      Three instruments sit behind these statements: the policy that mandates them,
      the Standard that sets their minimum content, and the APS AI Plan (the <a
        href={withBase("/whos-who")}>who&rsquo;s who</a> page sets out how they fit
      together). Where one of them asks a question, the profile asks it too; the
      remaining questions are ours. The usage patterns and domains are taken verbatim
      from the DTA&rsquo;s classification system. A question is a requirement only if
      its source makes it one, and the Chief AI Officer in particular comes from the
      APS AI Plan, not the policy.
    </p>
    <dl class="sources">
      {
        sources.map((source) => (
          <div>
            <dt>
              {FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL[source].href ? (
                <a href={FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL[source].href}>{FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL[source].long}</a>
              ) : (
                FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL[source].long
              )}
            </dt>
            <dd>
              <ul>
                {REQUIREMENTS.filter((q) => q.source === source).map((q) => (
                  <li>{q.question}</li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
    <p>
      Because the vocabularies are closed, two agencies&rsquo; profiles can be
      compared directly, and two revisions of one agency&rsquo;s profile can be
      diffed; the &ldquo;commitment dropped&rdquo; and &ldquo;Chief AI Officer:
      planned &rarr; in place&rdquo; markers come from those diffs. A bullet point
      expanded into a paragraph about the same thing produces no profile change. The
      profile records only what the statement itself says, and it records job titles
      but not people&rsquo;s names.
    </p>
    <p>
      The profile is a model&rsquo;s reading, and it can be wrong. A commitment
      worded differently in two revisions may appear as dropped and re-added, and a
      vaguely described role may be put in the wrong category. To limit this drift,
      each revision is read with the previous revision&rsquo;s profile as its
      baseline, and profile changes are reported only for revisions whose diff was
      judged worth reading. Every
      profile sits beside the statement it came from, and every change beside its
      diff, so both can be checked.
    </p>

    <h2 id="adoption">Adoption over time</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={withBase("/policy")}>policy in practice</a> page counts, for each
      month since tracking began, how many tracked statements&rsquo; profiles report each
      item (a Chief AI Officer, a register, mandatory training, a reference to version
      2.0) and shows that as a share of the statements tracked that month. Early
      months have small denominators: only {meta.counts.statements}{" "}
      statements are tracked now, and fewer were on day one. Agencies also joined in
      waves, so a jump from one month to the next can come from new entrants as
      easily as from new text. And a statement that mentions a register doesn&rsquo;t
      prove the register exists; the charts show what agencies <em>say</em>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two verdicts on that page rest on a statement&rsquo;s own dates.
      &ldquo;Updated since policy 2.0&rdquo; is true when the statement&rsquo;s
      self-declared last-updated date, or a change we observed ourselves, falls on or
      after 15 December 2025, and false when we have been watching since before then
      and have seen neither; where we can&rsquo;t tell, we say so.
      &ldquo;Overdue&rdquo; is simpler. The Standard requires a review at least once
      a year, so a statement whose own last-updated date is more than a year old is
      overdue by the agency&rsquo;s own account, and a statement with no date is
      marked neither current nor overdue. Both verdicts change as time passes, so
      both carry the date they were computed.
    </p>

    <h2 id="more">The fine print</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={withBase("/about")}>About page</a> has the technical method: how
      scrape glitches are filtered out, how passages are matched, which model does
      the reading, and the limits of the scraping itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because each statement is read from a live web page, a capture sometimes picks
      up the page around the statement as well, and the classifier has to tell the
      two apart. It usually does, but not always, so follow the source link on any
      statement to check the original.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .reading {
    max-width: 46rem;
  }

  .reading h3 {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-4);
  }

  .sources {
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .sources dt {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .sources dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .sources ul {
    margin: var(--space-1) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
</style>
